# NOETHERVIM: ORIENTATION

This is the first five minutes. It does not teach you to edit text; it tells
you what you are looking at, what happened when you opened a file, and the
four questions you can always ask the editor about itself.

Nothing here can damage your setup. A tutorial buffer is never written to
disk, so edit it freely.

Table of contents:

- [Reading this lesson](*howto*)
- [The dashboard](*dashboard*)
- [What happens when you open a file](*attach*)
- [The four questions](*questions*)
- [Where things live](*layout*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## READING THIS LESSON *howto*

Lines with a ✗ in the left margin are exercises; they turn into ✓ when the
text is right. Fix this one by changing `no` into `yes`.

I have read this far: no

Put the cursor on an indented command and press `>>` to run it. In an
ordinary buffer `>>` indents a line; inside a tutorial it executes one.

## THE DASHBOARD *dashboard*

Running `nvim` with no arguments opens a dashboard rather than an empty
buffer: a header and a short menu, each row showing the key that runs it.

    `f`   find a file          `o`   recent files
    `i`   new file, insert     `e`   new file, normal
    `r`   restore last session `s`   pick a session
    `c`   open your init.lua   `q`   quit

The session rows are the ones worth knowing early. NoetherVim remembers
where you were, so `r` puts back the windows and buffers you left.

You can come back to it at any time. `:Reset` closes every buffer and
returns you here, refusing if anything is unsaved; `:Reset!` discards
instead. If you would rather start in an empty buffer, set
`vim.g.noethervim_dashboard = false` in your init.lua; see
[the dashboard flag](noethervim-dashboard).

## WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU OPEN A FILE *attach*

Opening a file in a language NoetherVim knows sets up more than syntax
coloring, without being asked:

    a language server attaches, giving definitions, references and renames
    treesitter parses the file, giving structural highlighting and motions
    completion turns on, drawing on the server, the buffer and your snippets
    a formatter and a linter are selected for the filetype
    diagnostics start reporting in the margin and the statusline

If the server or the parser is not installed yet, which is normal on a fresh
install, it is fetched for you. That is what the activity in the corner
during your first few minutes is. To decline and manage toolchains yourself,
see [declining auto-install](noethervim-auto-install).

Two panels report on this. `<C-w><C-l>` shows what the language server is
doing, and `<C-w><C-m>` opens Mason, which is what installs the tools.

## THE FOUR QUESTIONS *questions*

Almost everything you will want to ask in the first week is one of four
questions, and each has one answer.

**What is bound?** `<Space>?` opens every active keymap, grouped by prefix,
read from the live state rather than a list someone maintained. `<CR>` on a
line jumps to the file that defined it.

**What can I turn on?** `<Space>cb` opens the bundle catalogue. NoetherVim
starts with no bundles enabled; the core is complete on its own.

**What else can this do?** `:NoetherVim` with no argument prints every
subcommand it has. Run it:

~~~ cmd
    :NoetherVim
~~~

**Is anything wrong?** `:checkhealth noethervim` reports your Neovim
version, terminal capabilities, missing tools, which of your own files
loaded, and whether any has drifted from what it overrides. Run it now and
read it; warnings here are worth meeting on purpose rather than later as a
bug.

~~~ cmd
    :checkhealth noethervim
~~~

Come back here with `[b` when you have read it, or reopen this lesson with
`:Tutor noethervim-01-orientation`.

Fill in the command that tells you whether anything is wrong:

The health check is ___

NOTE the terminal section of that report is a guess. A terminal cannot be
asked what it supports, so it goes by environment variables that can be
wrong. See [the terminal caveat](noethervim-health-terminal).

## WHERE THINGS LIVE *layout*

Three directories, and you only ever write in the second.

    the distribution   `lua/noethervim/`, read-only as far as you care
    your config        `lua/user/`, never touched by an update
    plugins            installed and pinned by lazy.nvim, managed for you

That split is the whole update story: upstream changes land in the first,
yours stay in the second, and nothing you wrote is overwritten.

You do not have a `lua/user/` yet.
[Lesson five](@tutor:noethervim-05-your-config) creates it.

